R=[
N('presidio-la-bahia-goliad','Presidio La Bahía','Goliad','tx','fort','standing',28.6475,-97.3825,'site','public','drive','forts','1749–1836',
  'The Spanish fort moved here, to the San Antonio River, in 1749 (Wikipedia gives 1747), after earlier sites on Matagorda Bay (1721) and the Guadalupe River (1726); by 1771 it had been rebuilt in stone, and the town of Goliad grew up round it. In the Texas Revolution it was taken in October 1835, and on Palm Sunday, March 27, 1836, Fannin\'s captured men held here were marched out and shot on Santa Anna\'s order: the Goliad massacre, behind the cry "Remember Goliad". A gift from Kathryn O\'Connor paid for a restoration in the 1960s that Wikipedia says rebuilt the fort almost from the ground up; it became a National Historic Landmark in 1967.',
  'The walled quadrangle and the chapel of Our Lady of Loreto, which the Texas Historical Commission calls the oldest building in the compound and in use since the 1700s; the fresco behind its altar was painted in 1946 by Antonio Garcia, and the statue over its door is by Lincoln Borglum. Museum inside. Open to the public: see the access note. The Zaragoza Birthplace (a reconstruction, run by the same agency) is next door.',
  'The fort where Fannin\'s men were held before the Goliad massacre.',
  'Operated by the Texas Historical Commission as a state historic site; Wikipedia says the property is owned by the Catholic Diocese of Victoria (ownership not checked further). 217 Loop 71, Goliad. Open daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., last admission 4:30; the THC page says admission is free through December 31 (2026), and lists $5 adults, $3 seniors and veterans, $2 children otherwise. Guided tours of the museum and chapel on Saturdays at 11 a.m. and 3 p.m. Parking at the site, not checked on the aerial.',
  [L('THC: Presidio La Bahía (hours, admission)','https://thc.texas.gov/historic-sites/presidio-la-bahia'),
   L('THC: Presidio La Bahía history','https://thc.texas.gov/state-historic-sites/presidio-la-bahia/presidio-la-bahia-history'),
   W('Presidio La Bahía'),L_GAHS],brick='no'),
N('mission-espiritu-santo-goliad','Mission Espíritu Santo','Goliad State Park','tx','inst','standing',28.65731,-97.38683,'exact','public','drive','','1749; rebuilt 1933–1941',
  'Mission Nuestra Señora del Espíritu Santo de Zúñiga was founded in 1722 on Matagorda Bay, moved to the Guadalupe River in 1726 and then here beside the presidio; its stone walls and buildings were not finished until 1758, and it ran one of the largest ranching operations in 18th-century Texas. After it closed, its stones were taken for building and the old church fell to ruin. The ruins went into the new state park in 1931; the Civil Works Administration began rebuilding the chapel and granary in 1933, and Civilian Conservation Corps Company 3822(V) finished the work between 1935 and 1941.',
  'The whitewashed mission church and granary as the 1930s crews rebuilt them, with exhibits on mission life (TPWD says some original items) and a bell visitors may ring. The large anacua tree by the mission entrance is put at nearly two centuries old. The chapel and granary are largely a 1930s reconstruction, not 18th-century fabric.',
  'A Spanish mission the Civilian Conservation Corps rebuilt from a ruin.',
  GSP+' 108 Park Road 6, Goliad. The mission is in the main park; where to park by it not checked on the aerial (the lot OpenStreetMap maps on Park Road 6 is a turning loop by the river).',
  [W('Mission Nuestra Señora del Espíritu Santo de Zúñiga'),L_GSP,L_GAHS,HM('Mission Espíritu Santo',235186)],brick='no'),
N('mission-rosario-goliad','Mission Rosario ruins','Goliad','tx','inst','ruins',28.644374,-97.438888,'site','view','drive','','1754–1831',
  'Mission Nuestra Señora del Rosario de los Cujanes was founded in 1754 for the Karankawa, about four miles west of the presidio. Its first buildings were timber and whitewashed clay, later stone and mortar, and at its peak it held 5,000 cattle; mismanagement and Karankawa resistance wore it down, it was almost empty by 1781, joined to Refugio in 1807 and finally secularized in 1831. Texas Parks and Wildlife dug the site for 24 weeks in 1972–73; a piece of wall with painted murals that was found is shown at Goliad State Park.',
  'Stone foundations outlining the mission buildings on a rise above the river, with a historical marker and bilingual interpretive panels. The Austin Chronicle says visitors cannot walk among the ruins and view them from behind a chain-link fence; the Handbook of Texas says the site is open only by guided tour. Look from the viewing area.',
  'Only the footings of the poorer sister mission are left.',
  'Mission Rosario State Historic Site, a unit of Goliad State Park run by Texas Parks and Wildlife (the TPWD page gives its position as 28.644374, -97.438888, on US 59 about 4 miles west of the park). Parking off US 59. Access rules differ between sources: see what to look for. Hours not checked separately from the main park.',
  [W('Mission Nuestra Señora del Rosario'),L('Handbook of Texas: Nuestra Señora del Rosario Mission','https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/nuestra-senora-del-rosario-mission'),
   L_GAHS,HM('Mission Rosario State Historic Site panels',235459),
   L('Austin Chronicle: Day Trips, Mission Rosario','https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-nuestra-senora-del-rosario-mission-11776296/')],
  conf='mixed',th=[28.64488,-97.43968],thn='Lot off US 59 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('fannin-memorial-goliad','Fannin Memorial Monument (grave of Fannin\'s men)','Goliad','tx','burial','marker',28.64609,-97.37967,'exact','view','walk','cemeteries','1836',
  'After the battle of Coleto in March 1836 about 400 of Fannin\'s men were shot outside Presidio La Bahía on March 27, and their bodies were partly burned and left. General Thomas J. Rusk\'s army later gathered the bones, carried them in procession from the presidio and gave them a military funeral here. The State of Texas monument over the grave lists the men killed at Coleto and those executed at Goliad, and says their remains lie beneath it.',
  'A monument with the names of the dead on its panels, just east of the presidio. The year it was put up is not given in the sources read.',
  'The common grave of the Goliad dead.',
  'Listed by Texas Parks and Wildlife among the Goliad historic sites, just east of Presidio La Bahía; who owns the ground and its hours not checked. Reached on foot from the presidio.',
  [HM('Grave of Colonel J. W. Fannin and His Men',35516),HM('Fannin Burial Monument',237668),L_GAHS,W('Goliad massacre')],brick='?'),
N('goliad-county-courthouse','Goliad County Courthouse and the Hanging Tree','Goliad','tx','civic','standing',28.66583,-97.39194,'exact','view','drive','','1894',
  'The fourth courthouse since the county was created in 1836, built in 1894 partly of limestone and brick from the old one (marker). It served as a hospital after the 1902 cyclone; a 1942 storm took off the central clock tower and the domed caps over the north and south doors. The Texas Historical Commission\'s courthouse program put back the towers, slate roof and cresting and restored the district courtroom, with SAH Archipedia giving 2003 for the work. On the lawn is the Hanging Tree, a live oak under which court sat at times from 1846 to 1870 and where sentences were carried out at once; men were also hanged on it during the 1857 Cart War.',
  'Brown-gray Muldoon sandstone walls, rusticated, with belt courses of dark red Pecos sandstone (SAH Archipedia); the clock tower and corner caps are 2000s replicas. The marker says carved staircases and walnut wainscots survive, and the district courtroom has a replica pressed metal ceiling, balconies and furniture. Whether visitors may go in, and when, not checked. The Hanging Tree is on the north lawn.',
  'A courthouse square with its own hanging tree.',
  'Goliad County courthouse, 127 North Courthouse Square; the square and the tree are public ground in the town. Street parking round the square. Hours for going inside not checked.',
  [HM('Goliad County Courthouse',132435),HM('The Hanging Tree',34113),
   L('SAH Archipedia: Goliad County Courthouse','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/47960'),
   L('THC: restored courthouse, Goliad County','https://thc.texas.gov/preserve/preservation-programs/courthouse-preservation/restored-historic-courthouses/goliad-county')],brick='?'),
N('market-house-museum-goliad','Market House Museum','Goliad','tx','civic','standing',28.66693,-97.39117,'exact','view','drive','','1871',
  'The town set the lot aside for public use in 1853, and about 1871 a market with stalls for meat and produce sellers went up on it. From 1886 it was the firehouse, with a meeting hall and a room for the hook and ladder; the false front and belfry date from an 1890 remodeling, and a bell bought that year rang the night hours and the alarms. The volunteer fire company left in 1964, and the building has been a museum since 1967.',
  'A small wooden building with a false front and belfry. Inside, exhibits on farming and ranching, with a wall of local cattle brands; TPWD says it gives out a walking-tour map of the courthouse square. Hours not checked.',
  'Market stall, then firehouse, then museum.',
  'Museum at 205 South Market Street, Goliad, a block from the courthouse square. Who owns it not checked. Street parking. Hours not checked: no page of the museum\'s own was found, so it is marked look-only until someone confirms when it opens.',
  [HM('Market House Museum',132241),L('SAH Archipedia: Market House Museum','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/47966'),
   L('Texas Time Travel: Market House Museum','https://texastimetravel.com/directory/market-house-museum-tour/'),L_GAHS],brick='no'),
N('bull-durham-sign-goliad','Bull Durham wall advertisement, Stout-Pettus block','Goliad','tx','landmark','standing',28.66562,-97.39275,'site','view','drive','','1894',
  'Goliad grew after the railroad came in 1889, and two buildings put up on the courthouse square in the boom have painted walls: the Stout-Pettus block of 1894, which carries this Bull Durham tobacco advertisement and one other, and the W. W. Denham building of 1900, with the same sign front and back. Bull Durham salesmen paid to have ads painted on the most prominent building in a town.',
  'The faded painted Bull Durham sign on the side wall of the Stout-Pettus block, beside the historical marker. The marker also names a third painted ad on a small barn at the northeast corner of Oak and Jefferson streets. Interior not checked.',
  'A tobacco ad painted onto the 1890s boom.',
  'Seen from the street on the courthouse square; the buildings are private businesses. The pin is the marker\'s position.',
  [HM('Bull Durham Tobacco Wall Advertisement',132544)],brick='?'),
N('fannin-battleground-shs','Fannin Battleground State Historic Site','Fannin','tx','fort','marker',28.68653,-97.23293,'exact','public','drive','','1836',
  'Here on March 19 and 20, 1836, Colonel James W. Fannin\'s Texian force, caught in the open on the retreat from Goliad, was surrounded by General José de Urrea\'s army on Coleto Creek and surrendered; Fannin and his men were shot at Goliad a week later. Goliad County residents gave the 14 acres to the state around 1913, and it was first called Fannin State Park.',
  'A stone obelisk where Fannin surrendered, an interpretive exhibit, a picnic pavilion, and a historic gin press screw first set up on the site in 1894. Nothing of 1836 stands.',
  'Where Fannin surrendered on the open prairie.',
  'Texas Historical Commission state historic site, 734 FM 2506, Fannin, about 10 miles east of Goliad. Open daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m., free. Parking on site; smoking only in the parking area, no alcohol.',
  [L('THC: Fannin Battleground (hours)','https://thc.texas.gov/historic-sites/fannin-battleground'),
   L('THC: Plan your visit','https://thc.texas.gov/state-historic-sites/fannin-battleground/plan-your-visit-fannin-battleground'),
   W('Fannin Battleground State Historic Site')],brick='no'),
N('old-helena-courthouse','Old Helena Courthouse (Karnes County Museum)','Helena','tx','town','standing',28.95011,-97.82037,'site','view','drive','','1873',
  'Helena, founded in 1852 on the San Antonio River by Thomas Ruckman and Lewis Owings, was the Karnes County seat from 1854, on a branch of the Chihuahua Trail freight road. After a storm and fire destroyed the first frame courthouse, this stone one was built in 1873 (the Texas Historical Commission names John Jacob Riley as architect). The railroad bypassed the town in 1886, Karnes City won the county seat in 1894, and the courthouse became a school; Helena is now close to a ghost town.',
  'The 1873 stone courthouse on the old square, which the Handbook of Texas says has been restored as a museum piece along with the old post office, the John Ruckman home, the Sickenius farmhouse and the jail; together they are the Karnes County Museum. Four markers stand round the square. Interior and opening hours not checked.',
  'The courthouse of a county seat the railroad killed.',
  'Karnes County Museum complex at the old courthouse square on FM 81 in Helena (8167 N FM 81 per Texas Time Travel). Who owns it and its hours not checked; see it from the road if closed.',
  [L('Handbook of Texas: Helena, TX','https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/helena-tx'),HM('Old Helena Courthouse',132370),HM('Helena',132367),
   L('THC: Karnes County craftsmanship','https://thc.texas.gov/blog/karnes-county-craftsmanship'),W('Helena, Texas'),
   L('Texas Time Travel: Karnes County Museum','https://texastimetravel.com/directory/karnes-county-museum/')],brick='no'),
N('panna-maria-church','Immaculate Conception Church, Panna Maria','Panna Maria','tx','worship','standing',28.9572,-97.89814,'exact','public','drive','','1877; school 1868',
  'Silesian Polish settlers led by Father Leopold Moczygemba reached this spot on Christmas Eve 1854 and held their first Mass under an oak; Panna Maria claims to be the oldest permanent Polish settlement in America. Their first church, consecrated in 1856, was destroyed by lightning in 1875, and this one with its 100-foot tower was built in 1877 and remodeled in 1937. In 2000 workers found that 1937 tiles hid a painted ceiling in the sanctuary, which was restored. St. Joseph\'s School nearby was built in 1868 and is called the oldest Polish private school in America.',
  'The white church with its tall tower and steeple (Wikipedia photograph) beside the oak of the first Mass. Inside: the restored painted ceiling, large stained-glass windows restored in 2013, and a mosaic of the Virgin of Czestochowa given by President Johnson in 1966. The Austin Chronicle says the church welcomes visitors daily during most daylight hours. Across the road are the 1868 school, the Polish Heritage Center and the visitors center in the old Pilarczyk store.',
  'The mother church of Polish America, with a ceiling found under tiles.',
  'An active Catholic parish church, 13879 FM 81, Panna Maria; be quiet during services. The Panna Maria visitors center gives its hours as Thursdays 10 to 3 (standard time) or 10 to 4 (daylight time) and weekends except holidays; the Austin Chronicle gives the Polish Heritage Center as Wednesday to Saturday 10 to 4. Church hours from the Austin Chronicle, not the parish. Parking at the church, not checked on the aerial.',
  [L('Panna Maria visitors center','https://pannamariatexas.com'),L('Handbook of Texas: Panna Maria, TX','https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/panna-maria-tx'),
   HM('Immaculate Conception Church',131921),HM("St. Joseph's School",131920),
   L('Austin Chronicle: Day Trips, Panna Maria','https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-panna-maria-texas-12945901'),W('Panna Maria, Texas')],brick='no'),
N('eckhardt-stores-yorktown','Eckhardt Stores','Yorktown','tx','industry','standing',28.98076,-97.50426,'exact','view','drive','','1876',
  'The Eckhardt family started a general store in 1848, soon after Yorktown was founded, hauling goods by wagon from the port of Indianola. William Eckhardt built this two-story store of thickly plastered native sandstone in 1876, with iron shutters on every door and window; a sandstone warehouse followed, and in 1895 it was taken into a two-story brick building sharing a wall with the store, when pine floors and a freight elevator were added. The 1976 Register form says the buildings had been given to the Yorktown Historical Society to keep as a local museum.',
  'Two joined two-story masonry store fronts on Main Street, the older of plastered sandstone and the 1895 one of brick, with the marker on the north wall of what HMdb calls the Yorktown Historical Building. The Yorktown museum gives its hours as Saturday 1 to 4 and Sunday 2 to 5 (not on holidays) and by appointment, but its page does not give its address: that it is still in this building is not checked. Interior not checked.',
  'A frontier store with iron shutters and walls two feet thick.',
  'Corner of West Main Street (State Highway 72) and South Eckhardt Street, Yorktown. Street parking. See it from the street; museum hours as above.',
  [L('National Register form (THC Atlas, PDF)','https://atlas.thc.state.tx.us/NR/pdfs/76002020/76002020.pdf'),HM('C. Eckhardt & Sons Building',174478),
   L('Yorktown museum hours','https://www.yorktowntx.com/museum')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('dewitt-county-courthouse-cuero','DeWitt County Courthouse','Cuero','tx','civic','standing',29.09184,-97.28877,'exact','view','drive','','1894–1896',
  'Built from 1894 to 1896 to a Romanesque Revival design by Arthur O. Watson, after the railroad had made Cuero the county town. In the 1950s the inside was stripped: tile over the Victorian detail, dropped ceilings and a new floor inserted into the district courtroom. A full restoration inside and out under the Texas Historical Commission\'s program put the courtroom back to its two-story height with its balcony, replicated the lost iron stairs and the woodwork, and the building was rededicated in October 2007.',
  'A three-story sandstone and granite courthouse with a six-story clock tower and flanking pavilions (HMdb marker). Inside, the restored two-story courtroom and replica iron stairs; whether visitors may go in, and when, not checked.',
  'A restored Romanesque courthouse.',
  'DeWitt County courthouse on the square in downtown Cuero. Street parking. Hours for going inside not checked.',
  [HM('De Witt County Courthouse',132624),
   L('THC: restored courthouse, DeWitt County','https://thc.texas.gov/preserve/preservation-programs/courthouse-preservation/restored-historic-courthouses/dewitt-county')],brick='?'),
N('karnes-county-courthouse','Karnes County Courthouse','Karnes City','tx','civic','standing',28.88521,-97.90153,'exact','view','drive','','1894',
  'When the San Antonio and Aransas Pass Railway bypassed Helena and ran through Karnes City, the county seat followed. John Cormack, who built courthouses in Concho, Somervell and Uvalde counties, won the contract over Alfred Giles and J. Riely Gordon; he died before it was done and a partner finished it. It was dedicated on October 25, 1894. From the 1920s it lost its clock tower and the conical roofs of its corner turrets, gained two additions and had stucco put over its brick; after restoration it was rededicated on April 7, 2018.',
  'A three-story brick courthouse with rusticated stone trim, mansard towers, round corner towers and a central clock tower, in Second Empire and Richardsonian Romanesque styles (HMdb marker, THC). Whether the stucco came off in the restoration, and whether visitors may go in, not checked.',
  'A courthouse that won the county seat from a ghost town.',
  'Karnes County courthouse on the square in Karnes City. Street parking. Hours for going inside not checked.',
  [HM('Karnes County Courthouse',292027),L('THC: Karnes County craftsmanship','https://thc.texas.gov/blog/karnes-county-craftsmanship')],brick='?'),
]
